Modified Context:
Genes can acquire mutations in their sequence, leading to identical variants, known as alleles, in the population. These alleles encode slightly different versions of a protein, which cause different phenotype traits. Colloquial usage of the term "having a gene" (e.g., "good genes," "hair color gene") typically refers to having an identical variant of the gene. Genes evolve due to natural selection or survival of the fittest of the alleles.

